-- The object language: a toy ML, small enough to read in one sitting.
--
-- Everything the interpreter knows how to say is in this file. `Expr` is what
-- the parser builds, the checker types and the evaluator runs -- one tree, three
-- passes, which is the whole shape of the thing.

-- An expression.
--
-- `Expr.LetRec` is separate from `Expr.Let` because the two type differently: a `let`
-- generalizes its right-hand side *before* the body sees it, while a `let rec`
-- has to have the name in scope, monomorphically, while checking the right-hand
-- side. That distinction is the interesting part of Hindley-Milner, so it is
-- worth having both rather than one clever case.
@pub(pkg) data Expr
  = Int Int
  | Bool Bool
  | Var String
  | Lam String Expr
  | App Expr Expr
  | Let String Expr Expr
  | LetRec String String Expr Expr
  | If Expr Expr Expr
  | Bin String Expr Expr

-- A type. `Ty.Var` is a type variable, by its number in the union-find that
-- inference keeps, which is what makes this Algorithm J rather than
-- Algorithm W: unification writes the answer into the variable's group
-- instead of building a substitution to apply later.
@pub(pkg) data Ty = Int | Bool | Fun Ty Ty | Var Int

-- What a group of type variables, unified together, knows about itself.
--
-- `Unbound level` -- not yet solved. The `level` is the `let`-nesting depth
-- at which it was created, and it is how generalization stays cheap: a variable
-- may be quantified exactly when its level is deeper than the binding being
-- generalized, which is checkable without scanning the environment.
--
-- `Bound t` -- solved, to `t`, which is never a variable itself: two variables
-- unified are one group, not one bound to the other.
@pub(pkg) data Tv = Unbound Int | Bound Ty

-- A type scheme: `forall ids. ty`. The ids are the unsolved variables that
-- were generalized; `instantiate` swaps each for a fresh variable.
@pub(pkg) data Scheme = Forall (Vector Int) Ty
